			"id": "T0QY4_uyNHjB6mAG-",
			"name": "Body Lens: Destroyer",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Destroyers (and the more radically converted Skorpekh Destroyers) are Necrons whose minds fractured, during or after the biotransfer- ence, into a consuming hatred of all organic life; in exchange their bodies were rebuilt into heavy, part-mechanised war-forms far removed from a humanoid chassis. Codex background across editions is consistent that Destroyers are shunned even by other Necrons and answer only reluctantly to central dynastic authority.\n\nDesign: 296 (Attribute Modifiers) + 10 (Basic Move +2) + 500 (Necrodermis DR 100, 5/level) = 806 points, added to Necron Physiology's 224 for 1,030 points of physiology and body alone, before the Mind: Destroyer Cult package and skills. See the Skorpekh Destroyer template. This lens is a reasoned extrapolation above the Noble anchor (ST 36 on framework.md's strength ladder), not a framework.md Necron table row; a future framework update that adds an explicit Destroyer anchor should supersede it.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tasz8MeI4P3bc9RQJ",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Destroyer)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Not one of framework.md's own anchor rows -- the framework's Necron table stops at Cryptek, and Skorpekh Destroyers are extrapolated here rather than anchored: a Warrior- or Immortal- grade mind fused permanently into a heavy, scything mechanised war-form, so its body should sit above Lychguard and Overlord on raw ST and HT (a bigger chassis than any biped) while its mind stays as debased as a Warrior's or worse (the Destroyer Cult mind package below). Increased ST +26 (B14, 10/level, 260) for ST 36. Increased DX +2 (40) for DX 12, matching a Lychguard's precision despite the bulk. Decreased IQ -4 (-80) for IQ 6, as fractured as a Warrior's mind, per the framework's own description of Destroyer Cultists as having lost themselves to hatred. Increased HT +6 (B15, 10/level, 60) for HT 16 -- a war-engine's resilience. Extra Hit Points +8 (16) on top of the ST-36 base of 36, for HP 44. 260+40-80+60+16 = 296.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 296,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 26
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 6
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 8
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tE2Q2rfd3U__34-yP",
					"name": "Increased Basic Move (Destroyer)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Skorpekh war-form strides and lunges on multiple limbs with a speed that belies its bulk; Destroyers close on prey far faster than a Warrior's plodding advance.\n\nDesign: Increased Basic Move +2 (B17, 5/level, 10 points) for Move 9 (Basic Speed 7.0 gives Move 7 before this purchase).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 10,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tbGZQDsIpozVydPtY",
					"name": "Necrodermis (Destroyer)",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 100, all locations -- as heavy as a Lord's own plate, per the fiction's treatment of Destroyers as very hard to put down. Weak Points 3 (a fused mechanised war-form has far fewer exposed seams than a humanoid chassis): on a hit that fails to penetrate, roll 3d; 3 or less halves DR (50). Eye-slit optics run at DR 50 rather than 100.\n\nLore: Necrodermis is a Necron's body and its armour at once: a skin of near-indestructible living metal with no separate suit to strip away. Universal across Codex background and every piece of Black Library Necron fiction.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance (B46, 5/level, all locations): natural DR bought directly on the body, per framework.md's explicit instruction that necrodermis is a character trait, not equipment. Each body-type lens overrides `levels`/`dr` to its own figure from framework.md's Necron anchor table; see that lens's own copy of this trait for its point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 100,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 100
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "T20p-9xMbu92aDDKh",
			"name": "Mind: Slave Mentality",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Most Necrons who went through the biotransference lost themselves almost entirely -- Warriors above all, who function as an obedient tool rather than a soldier with opinions.\n\nDesign: -40 (Slave Mentality) - 1 (Automaton Discipline) = -41 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t90y5coAylIOjhLU0",
					"name": "Slave Mentality",
					"reference": "B154",
					"local_notes": "As Slave Mentality (B154), no self-control roll: defers to any perceived authority, cannot act on its own initiative against orders, and struggles to disobey even a bad order from its Overlord's court.\n\nLore: A Warrior's mind is not merely dim, it is built to obey: Codex background treats Warriors as automatons that fight exactly as ordered and stop being useful in a fight the instant central command breaks down, per the tabletop convention that a Warrior unit without an active officer nearby is far less reliable.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Slave Mentality (B154), -40 points, no self-control roll. For a softer obedience use Reprogrammable (-10, B150) plus Duty instead.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -40
				},
				{
					"id": "txWZotsS7ssIIU3YA",
					"name": "Automaton Discipline",
					"local_notes": "Roleplaying flag only.\n\nLore: A Warrior defaults to its last-given order or a simple guard/patrol drill rather than improvising, even when improvising would obviously serve better.\n\nDesign: Quirk (B9), roleplaying flag only.",
					"tags": [
						"Quirk",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TzB2-42mSl704RoOC",
			"name": "Mind: Immortal Discipline",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Immortals are dim but capable of real tactical thought, one step up from a Warrior's blank obedience and nowhere near a noble's full personality.\n\nDesign: -5 (Sense of Duty) - 1 (Rote Thinker) = -6 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tncswqzSrQY641Er8",
					"name": "Sense of Duty (The Dynasty)",
					"reference": "B153",
					"local_notes": "Small group -- the dynasty's ruling court and its stated goals, not the whole species.\n\nLore: Immortals hold enough of themselves to serve with real, if narrow, loyalty rather than pure programmed obedience -- Codex background's usual framing of Immortals as trusted, disciplined elite guard.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Sense of Duty (B153), small group, -5 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -5
				},
				{
					"id": "tmAah_BBqMlqO5mAq",
					"name": "Rote Thinker",
					"local_notes": "Roleplaying flag only.\n\nLore: An Immortal follows doctrine to the letter and is visibly uncomfortable improvising outside it.\n\nDesign: Quirk (B9), roleplaying flag only.",
					"tags": [
						"Quirk",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TjpiKgO0ezpWUt4C4",
			"name": "Mind: Deathmark Focus",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Deathmarks keep just enough of a mind to hunt alone and adapt to a single target, and nothing resembling a social self beyond that.\n\nDesign: 5 (Single-Minded) - 10 (No Sense of Humor) = -5 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tcTHzSCPab81a0GjK",
					"name": "Single-Minded",
					"reference": "B85",
					"local_notes": "Represents a Deathmark's total, undistractable focus on its assigned target.\n\nLore: A Deathmark exists to track and kill one target with total commitment, undistracted by anything else on the battlefield -- the defining trait of the caste across Codex background.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Single-Minded (B85), 5 points, the same entry this project's Astartes Hypno-Indoctrination package uses.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 5
				},
				{
					"id": "thTkl6XzdFr1QT2CT",
					"name": "No Sense of Humor",
					"reference": "B146",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Deathmark has essentially no personality left to banter with -- minimal, cold, purpose-built.\n\nDesign: Basic Set No Sense of Humor (B146), -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -10
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TPQsc6sp80nqRtHhe",
			"name": "Deathmark Phase-Shift",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Deathmark hunts from phase: partly out of step with reality, it arrives unseen and turns a shot aside as it lands.\n\nDesign: 12 (Ethereal Interception) + 6 (Phase-In Ambush) = 18 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tptB34SfEE3jSRDpL",
					"name": "Ethereal Interception",
					"reference": "B51",
					"local_notes": "+1 to Dodge against ranged attacks: the Deathmark phases partly out of reality as a shot arrives.\n\nLore: Deathmarks are described in the Codex as partially phased out of reality between shots, which is why fire aimed at them seems to pass through the moment before it lands.\n\nDesign: Enhanced Dodge (B51, 15/level) level 1, Limited to ranged attacks (-20%): 15 x 0.8 = 12 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mIwCDxazudupvOHnk",
							"name": "Limited (ranged attacks only)",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						}
					],
					"points_per_level": 15,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				},
				{
					"id": "t57DpbyRiWqMMLpdP",
					"name": "Phase-In Ambush",
					"local_notes": "Once per scene, while out of sight of every enemy, the Deathmark may appear at any spot within 20 yards that it could see if it were standing there. It cannot move through solid barriers, and acts normally next turn.\n\nLore: Deathmarks are described arriving from the ambush position of a dimensional pocket or phase-shift and opening fire with no warning, rather than marching to a firing position.\n\nDesign: House price of 6 points, since the Basic Set library has no teleportation trait; one use per scene, out of sight only.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 6
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "THvjPajy4JNS-Z-KQ",
			"name": "Mind: Lychguard Honour",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Lychguard kept enough of themselves to serve with real, ritualised devotion, without the full independent personality of a noble.\n\nDesign: -10 (Code of Honor) - 5 (Sense of Duty) = -15 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tFoYiGSt1hPYtFV7O",
					"name": "Code of Honor (Necron Nobility)",
					"reference": "B127",
					"local_notes": "Never abandon your charge; never break formation without leave; settle any grudge against another Lychguard through ritual challenge, not the battlefield.\n\nLore: Lychguard fight and die by an ancient bodyguard's code as old as the dynasties themselves -- Crowley's Twice-Dead King plays this as genuine, not decorative.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Code of Honor (Soldier's)-style build (B127), -10 points, reflavored to the Necron nobility's own ancient code.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -10
				},
				{
					"id": "tOJaDbCYNHnQSZLKX",
					"name": "Sense of Duty (Their Charge)",
					"reference": "B153",
					"local_notes": "Single individual -- the specific Lord or Overlord this Lychguard is bound to protect.\n\nLore: A Lychguard's loyalty is bound to one noble, not the dynasty in the abstract.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Sense of Duty (B153), single individual, -5 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -5
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TMDuAiOOO7AQWXZLa",
			"name": "Mind: Flayer Curse",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Flayer Curse is a degeneration of a Necron's reanimation matrix that erases whatever personality survived the biotransference and replaces it with a single, murderous compulsion.\n\nDesign: -10 (Bloodlust) - 15 (Compulsive Behavior) - 10 (Social Stigma) = -35 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tcO1fKEDI38im54cA",
					"name": "Bloodlust",
					"reference": "B125",
					"local_notes": "As Bloodlust, but specifically the compulsion to flay and take the flesh of a defeated foe.\n\nLore: The Flayer Curse turns a Necron's reanimation-matrix corruption into a compulsion to kill and flay, over and over -- Codex background's core description of Flayed Ones.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Bloodlust (B125), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "t5HnvOdyYHcX8e65C",
					"name": "Compulsive Behavior (Flesh-Taking)",
					"reference": "B128",
					"local_notes": "Must wear or carry flayed skin taken personally; goes without it only under duress, and poorly.\n\nLore: Flayed Ones are consistently shown draped in the skins of their victims, a compulsion rather than a fashion choice.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Compulsive Behavior (B128), self-control 12, -15 points (Serious tier, since it is the caste's whole defining trait).",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -15,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tB_5NIq8ciOEooBRv",
					"name": "Social Stigma (Cursed Outcast)",
					"reference": "B155",
					"local_notes": "Shunned and quarantined by other Necrons, including their own dynasty, as a contamination risk.\n\nLore: Even other Necrons keep Flayed Ones at arm's length, Codex background treating the Flayer Curse as a contagion-like degeneration rather than a lifestyle choice.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Social Stigma (B155), -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Social",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -10
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "T2kHyUP9xgxT3gH-5",
			"name": "Mind: Noble Personality",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Necron nobility kept its personality through the biotransference intact, for better or worse: proud, scheming, often eccentric after sixty million years alone with its own thoughts, and bound by codes of honour and duty as old as the dynasty itself.\n\nDesign: -5 (Overconfidence) - 10 (Obsession) - 10 (Code of Honor) + 15 (Status 3) = -10 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tdEaeaJheqJLU67b3",
					"name": "Overconfidence",
					"reference": "B148",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A noble who ruled worlds before the Imperium existed rarely doubts that it can still do so -- Rath's The Infinite and the Divine builds an entire novel around exactly this flaw in two Necron nobles.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Overconfidence (B148), self-control 12, -5 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -5,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tR58q44V3Y4B6tASr",
					"name": "Obsession (Restore the Dynasty's Glory)",
					"reference": "B146",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Nearly every Necron noble in the fiction is driven by some version of the same goal: reclaim what the dynasty lost across sixty million years of sleep, whether that means territory, artefacts, or simple respect.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Obsession (B146), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "t29dPPIwYftE0gQ1K",
					"name": "Code of Honor (Necron Nobility)",
					"reference": "B127",
					"local_notes": "Honour debts and grudges are settled personally and openly; a noble does not go back on a given word to another noble, whatever it costs.\n\nLore: The old dynastic codes of conduct predate the Imperium and are still observed, however cynically, by nobles across the fiction.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Code of Honor (B127), -10 points, the same base entry as the Lychguard's own Code, reflavored for a ruler rather than a bodyguard.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -10
				},
				{
					"id": "taaxovgvR6AHo4KGn",
					"name": "Status (Necron Nobility)",
					"reference": "B28",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Lord or Overlord genuinely outranks everything around it, in a hierarchy far older than any human institution.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Status 3 (B28, 5/level, 15 points).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Social",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TyuhlnZjiWKJdZJ6E",
			"name": "Mind: Cryptek Genius",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Cryptek kept its full mind and sharpened it further -- genuinely brilliant, and single-mindedly curious about the half-understood technology it wields.\n\nDesign: -5 (Curious) - 10 (Obsession) = -15 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t_UY4I4A4a9wX_ErR",
					"name": "Curious",
					"reference": "B129",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Cryptek investigates first and considers the consequences later -- every depiction of Crypteks in the fiction shows them unable to leave an interesting anomaly alone.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Curious (B129), self-control 12, -5 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -5,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tlct8N-ya7mgR24Gn",
					"name": "Obsession (Unlock the Tomb-World's Secrets)",
					"reference": "B146",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Whatever a specific Cryptek's discipline, the underlying drive in the fiction is the same: recover and master pre- biotransference science the rest of the dynasty has lost or forgotten.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Obsession (B146), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "T9tp9NpGBvfBkwOlC",
			"name": "Mind: Destroyer Cult",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Destroyer Cult is what remains of a Necron mind that broke, not into obedience like a Warrior's or a compulsion like a Flayed One's, but into a pure, undifferentiated hatred of every living thing -- shunned by the rest of the dynasty and only loosely answerable to central command.\n\nDesign: -10 (Intolerance) - 15 (Berserk) - 10 (Bloodlust) = -35 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t3rjOTh9CiJenqsP1",
					"name": "Intolerance (All Organic Life)",
					"reference": "B140",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Destroyer Cultists have fractured into a hatred that does not distinguish one living species from another -- Codex background across editions treats this as total and undiscriminating.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Intolerance (B140), broad category, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -10
				},
				{
					"id": "tMT5gXzgOj7FTR1rd",
					"name": "Berserk",
					"reference": "B124",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Destroyer that gets a clear shot at organic life is consist- ently shown pressing the attack past the point of sense, per the caste's core fictional identity.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Berserk (B124), self-control 12, -15 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -15,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tQ7CE51BY9w9WA5rS",
					"name": "Bloodlust",
					"reference": "B125",
					"local_notes": "As Bloodlust, but omnicidal rather than personal: any living thing will do.\n\nLore: A Destroyer does not need a reason to kill a living target, only an opportunity.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Bloodlust (B125), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TB0x7MlA5l8yY3ofV",
			"name": "Cryptek Discipline: Technomancy",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Technomancers specialise in the half-understood engineering side of Necron science: reweaving damaged necrodermis, jury-rigging constructs, and keeping a dynasty's ancient machines running.\n\nDesign: 14 (Mending Field, level 2) + 10 (Artificer's Eye, level 1) = 24 points at default levels.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tGdJtWbI-5S8Ys6Oy",
					"name": "Mending Field",
					"reference": "B59",
					"local_notes": "Touch-range repair, restoring HP-equivalent structural damage to necrodermis or other machines only; has no effect on living tissue.\n\nLore: A Technomancer's signature trick: laying a hand on damaged necrodermis (a comrade's or their own) and knitting the metal back into shape well beyond its own passive self-repair rate.\n\nDesign: Healing (B59, 10 points/level) at level 2 (20 points) with Accessibility (constructs/necrodermis only, -30%, an eyeballed percentage per this project's own Accessibility convention): 20 x 0.7 = 14 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mC_JA9-n2E-ZFf4rD",
							"name": "Accessibility (necrodermis and other constructs only)",
							"cost_adj": "-30%"
						}
					],
					"points_per_level": 10,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2
				},
				{
					"id": "tZLpBvHX7gn9KuNIE",
					"name": "Artificer's Eye",
					"reference": "B90",
					"local_notes": "Adds its level to Engineer (Necrodermis), Mechanic (Necron Constructs) and Armoury (Necron Weapons).\n\nLore: A Technomancer's instinct for Necron machinery runs deeper than formal training could teach a human engineer.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting three skills, Medium Talent rate (B90, 10/level, 3-5 skill bracket), the same rate this project's Mek Instinct entry uses. Level 1, 10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Talent",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"points_per_level": 10,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				}
			],
			"disabled": true
		},
		{
			"id": "TuYrRbosCRDTOEbeo",
			"name": "Cryptek Discipline: Plasmancy",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Plasmancers are Necron energy-weapons specialists, channelling the same particle science behind gauss weaponry into more focused, more destructive bursts.\n\nDesign: 60 points (Channelled Discharge, level 6 with Armor Divisor (3)) at default level.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tMBfvCCeptX0S2VdT",
					"name": "Channelled Discharge",
					"reference": "B61",
					"local_notes": "6d(3) burn: an emergency discharge straight from the hands, matching the staff-of-light tier. A Plasmancer normally releases this through a staff or rod (built separately as equipment) rather than empty-handed.\n\nLore: Plasmancers store and release raw particle/plasma energy through their staves, and can (if disarmed) still discharge a dangerous burst directly from their own hands as a last resort.\n\nDesign: Innate Attack (Burning) (B61, 5 points/die), the same anchor this project's 'Eadbanger and Betcher's Gland entries use. Level 6 (30 points) with Armor Divisor (3) (B102, +100%): 30 x 2 = 60 points. Raised from 4d with no divisor, which left a Plasmancer weaker than an ordinary Warrior's gauss flayer; 6d(3) matches the Staff of Light's own ranged tier.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mfI1wV9T1X5I-6ryt",
							"name": "Armor Divisor (3)",
							"reference": "B102",
							"cost_adj": "+100%"
						}
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 6
				}
			],
			"disabled": true
		},
		{
			"id": "Tqg31dw0acxDBMfOo",
			"name": "Cryptek Discipline: Psychomancy",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Psychomancers weaponise dread and despair through Necron tech rather than the warp -- close to a psyker's toolkit in effect, nothing like one in mechanism.\n\nDesign: 16 points (Aura of Dread) at default level.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tWVkMGEkiFg4P9s5e",
					"name": "Aura of Dread",
					"reference": "B35",
					"local_notes": "Anyone within 2 yards of the Psychomancer's despair-generator scarabs suffers -2 to Fright Checks. Technological, not psychic: unaffected by Warp Sorcery countermeasures.\n\nLore: Psychomancers carry technology (usually swarms of tiny despair- generator scarabs) that projects a crushing dread rather than any true psychic power -- Codex background is explicit that Necron \"psychic\" effects are engineered, not warp-born.\n\nDesign: Affliction (B35) base 10, Disadvantage Delivered (Fright Check -2, valued at +30% per this project's own Null Aura precedent in data/imperium/psykers/warp.yaml), Area Effect (2 yards, +50%, B102) and Emanation (-20%, B111): net +60%. 10 x 1.6 = 16 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mo6AmKH_Ee0pOkbtO",
							"name": "Disadvantage Delivered (Fright Check -2)",
							"cost_adj": "+30%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m1NC7dyDiIk6scabi",
							"name": "Area Effect, 2 yards",
							"cost_adj": "+50%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mdZGiFq1DBZGKINGU",
							"name": "Emanation (no ranged attack roll, proximity only)",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				}
			],
			"disabled": true
		},
		{
			"id": "TfbpXkJC5kOHlT2iL",
			"name": "Cryptek Discipline: Chronomancy",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Chronomancers are the rarest and strangest Cryptek discipline, manipulating probability and small pockets of local time rather than matter or energy directly.\n\nDesign: 15 (Chronal Precognition) + 9 (Temporal Distortion Field) = 24 points at default levels.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tSwVMr3a9SJXIchYn",
					"name": "Chronal Precognition",
					"reference": "B47",
					"local_notes": "Reflavored Danger Sense: a Chronomancer reads the immediate probability-stream around itself for incoming danger a fraction of a second before it happens.\n\nLore: Chronomancers manipulate probability and localised time rather than true prophecy, and the most reliable payoff of that is knowing danger is coming an instant before it arrives.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Danger Sense (B47, 15 points), reflavored.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "tWzwktSlcpsDiO6XD",
					"name": "Temporal Distortion Field",
					"reference": "B35",
					"local_notes": "A touch that locally slows a target's passage through time: -1 to Basic Speed (and so to Dodge and Move) for the rest of the encounter unless removed by a GM-adjudicated effect.\n\nLore: A Chronomancer's staff can pin a small volume of space slightly out of step with the current moment, dragging on anyone caught in it. Codex background frames Chronomancer power as subtle battlefield-tempo manipulation rather than flashy destruction.\n\nDesign: Affliction (B35) base 10, Disadvantage Delivered (Decreased Basic Speed 1, valued at roughly +5% per this project's own eyeballed Disadvantage Delivered convention) and Melee Attack, Reach C only (-20%, a touch-range limitation): net -15%. 10 x 0.85 = 8.5, rounded up to 9 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mcZ511BGQTCiuoIpc",
							"name": "Disadvantage Delivered (Decreased Basic Speed 1)",
							"cost_adj": "+5%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mkO7Q43I17gPhTkYv",
							"name": "Melee Attack, Reach C only",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				}
			],
			"disabled": true
		}
	]
}
